  "worked_solution": "\u0410 second-order linear differential equation has the form\n\n$$\na(x){y}^{\u2033}+b(x){y}^{\u2032}+c(x)y=f(x).\n$$\n\nLet's look at each equation in turn.\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n(\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x){y}^{\u2033}-x{y}^{\u2032}+y=2\n$$\n\nis a second-order linear ODE with $a(x)=\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x,b(x)=-x,c(x)=1,$ and $f(x)=2.$\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{3}{y}^{\u2033}+x{y}^{\u2032}-xy=0\n$$\n\nis **NOT** linear because it contains the nonlinear term ${y}^{3}{y}^{\u2033}.$\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{x}{y}^{\u2033}+xy{y}^{\u2032}+{x}^{2}y={e}^{x}\n$$\n\nis **NOT** linear because it contains the nonlinear term $xy{y}^{\u2032}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"",
